! LOAD CONVERTED (LCV), the decimal-to-binary direction.  Reference
! Manual printed p.72:
!
!   "The addressed storage field is converted as an integer from
!   decimal to binary or from binary to decimal depending on the
!   setting of the radix modifier.  THE ACCUMULATOR IS CLEARED AND
!   THE CONVERTED FIELD IS PLACED INTO IT AT THE SPECIFIED OFFSET.
!   The four low-order bits of the sign byte register are cleared.
!   The sign bit in the sign byte register is set according to the
!   sign of the data as affected by the sign modifiers."
!
! and, under Radix on the same page, the sentence that unblocked this
! whole operation:
!
!   "Bit 53 of the instruction, the radix modifier, specifies the
!   radix of the OPERAND TO BE CONVERTED, and hence also whether
!   binary-to-decimal or decimal-to-binary conversion is to be
!   performed.  If this bit is zero, the original field is binary; if
!   this bit is one, the original field is decimal."
!
! THE MODEL USED TO REFUSE LCV OUTRIGHT, because the VFL arm sets
! Operation Code Invalid on the decimal modifier across the board and
! decimal arithmetic is unimplemented.  But for a radix conversion
! that bit is not "do decimal arithmetic" -- printed p.71 says it
! "describes the format of the ORIGINAL field and also determines the
! type of conversion" -- so refusing it refused the operation's
! normal case.  Decimal ARITHMETIC is still refused; only LCV is let
! past.
!
! p.73's top adds the byte-size rule: the decimal field is converted
! "to byte size four" BEFORE the conversion, so a byte wider than
! four contributes its four low-order bits and a narrower one is
! zero-extended.
!
! ARRIVED VIA MCP, printed p.17 line 34, "LCV(DU,8,4),$LZC+.3,46
! -CONVERT TO OCTAL NUMBER": the conversion that turns an IOCD card's
! Hollerith channel digits into a channel number.  It is the LAST of
! the two operations standing between MCP and knowing what devices it
! has; KF, the other, was built earlier the same day.  Blocks 1 and 2
! are MCP's own shape, eight bits at byte size four -- two decimal
! digits -- at offset 0 and at MCP's own offset of 46.
!
! ONLY DECIMAL-TO-BINARY IS BUILT.  The other direction wants
! repeated division by ten and nothing in the corpus calls for it; a
! binary source sets OP rather than being silently wrong.
!
!   T1  MCP's shape at offset 0: BCD 37 -> 37.
!   T2  the same at MCP's own offset 46, so the value lands at
!       accumulator bit 46 and reads back as 37 << 46.  A model that
!       ignored the offset would put 37 here.
!   T3  six digits: BCD 123456 -> 123456, which no two-digit case
!       could distinguish from a byte-wise copy.
!   T4  a SIGNED decimal field.  The sign byte is the RIGHTMOST byte
!       and lives inside the field length (p.56), so (D,12,4) is two
!       digits over a four-bit sign byte: 0x378 is 37 with the sign
!       nibble's leading bit set.  The magnitude is 37 and the
!       arithmetic result indicators say NEGATIVE.
!   T5  the same field with the N modifier on an UNSIGNED read, which
!       is the other way to reach a negative result -- "unsigned
!       fields are considered positive before this modifier is
!       applied" (p.60, via p.72's "these modifiers operate as in
!       ADD").  LCVN is spelled with C22-6531 footnote 4's N suffix.
!
! THE INDICATOR BLOCKS RUN THEIR OWN LCV.  A store sets MOP and the
! arithmetic result indicators, so reading the register after an ST
! would be reading the ST.  Z,$IND then LCV then load the register is
! the only ordering that reports the conversion alone -- and LCV
! needs no clearing load before it, because it clears the accumulator
! itself.
!
! Expected indicator word for a negative result: RLZ = 0x80 plus
! RN = 0x10, and nothing else -- MOP off, PF off, the field being
! well under fifteen digits.

! TWO INJECTIONS, MEASURED, and they separate the conversion from the
! placement:
!   the conversion replaced by a raw copy of the digit bits fails
!     FIVE value checks -- 55 for 37, 1193046 for 123456 -- and
!     NEITHER indicator check, because a BCD field read as binary is
!     still negative and still not fifteen digits long.
!   the offset ignored fails T2 ALONE, and fails it to exactly T1's
!     value, which is what makes running the same conversion at two
!     offsets worth the four extra instructions.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

! -- 1. MCP's shape, offset 0 ------------------------------------
START   LCV(DU,8,4), SRC37+.56, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T1, 0

! -- 2. MCP's own offset ------------------------------------------
        LCV(DU,8,4), SRC37+.56, 46
        ST(BU,64,8), T2, 0

! -- 3. six digits -------------------------------------------------
        LCV(DU,24,4), SRC6+.40, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T3, 0

! -- 4. a signed decimal field: magnitude, then the indicators -----
        LCV(D,12,4), SRCN+.52, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T4, 0
        Z, $IND
        LCV(D,12,4), SRCN+.52, 0
        L(BU,64,8), $IND, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T4IND, 0

! -- 5. the N modifier on an unsigned read -------------------------
        LCVN(DU,8,4), SRC37+.56, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T5, 0
        Z, $IND
        LCVN(DU,8,4), SRC37+.56, 0
        L(BU,64,8), $IND, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T5IND, 0

PASS    BEW, $

! Data, word-aligned: BEW is a half word, so without this the 64-bit
! fields below would straddle two words.
        SLC, (8)2040.0

! BCD 37 in the low byte.
SRC37   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000067
! BCD 123456 in the low 24 bits.
SRC6    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000004432126
! BCD 37 over a negative four-bit sign byte, in the low 12 bits.
SRCN    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000001570

T1      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T2      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T3      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T4      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T4IND   DD(BU,64,8), 0
T5      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T5IND   DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, START
